# Content Summary Rule

## Every new content table must have a `summary TEXT` column

Any table that stores a piece of content (outputs, documents, tasks, notes, discussions, research entries, etc.) must include:

```sql
summary TEXT  -- nullable; populated at write time
```

This column is the **embedding anchor** for future RAG / semantic search. Without it, content cannot be meaningfully linked to business plan sections, briefs, or other context later.

## The summary must be populated when content is created or significantly edited

Not at read time. Not lazily. At write time — in the API route that creates or updates the content row.

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — summary derived from content at save time
const summary = deriveOutputSummary(brief, content, contentTypeName)
await createOutput({ ..., summary })

// ❌ BAD — no summary, content becomes an island
await createOutput({ brief, content })
```

## What a good summary looks like

A summary is **not** the first N characters of the content body. It is a concise description of:
- What kind of content this is
- What topic, brief, or objective it addresses
- What the content says or concludes

```
// ✅ GOOD — describes the content for retrieval
"LinkedIn post: Positioning SPLYTS as the training intelligence leader for S&C coaches. Athletes train in cycles — why doesn't their programme intelligence?"

// ❌ BAD — meaningless truncation
"Here's your draft:\n\nAthletes train in cycles"
```

A good summary is 1–3 sentences, 100–500 characters, and reads naturally as a standalone description.

## How to derive a summary

**For AI-generated content** (outputs): derive synchronously at save time from `brief` + opening lines of `content`. No extra AI call needed — the brief already describes the intent.

**For user-written content** (documents, notes): use the document's title + first 2 sentences on save.

**For discussions**: use the `ai_summary` extracted at resolution time (already implemented).

**For structured data** (KPI snapshots, goals): combine the period label + filled fields. A single sentence is sufficient.

## When the RAG pipeline is extended

When `retrieveRelevantDocuments` is extended to new content types, the `summary` column is what gets embedded — not the raw body. Do not embed raw body content directly; always prefer the summary.

## Hard rules

- No new content table without a `summary TEXT` column
- No content creation route that does not populate `summary`
- No `summary` that is simply `content.slice(0, N)` — it must describe the content meaningfully
- Summary is nullable (AI may be unavailable) but must be attempted at write time — do not skip
